Comment: @Kyle, I think your statement about "not as bad (as bad), is as bad(bad), and the last being(being) is even better" is actually a really poor example.  "Not as bad is as bad" suggests that a person who doesn't like the last thing is less likely to dislike the second thing.  In English, at least, it always implies that a person who does like the last thing is more likely to dislike the second thing.  ("Not as bad is as bad" would become true if the person who liked the second thing, disliked the last thing, and the person who disliked the second thing, liked the last thing.)

